/*
# Card — a themed surface with its content inset

The box everything else sits on: the theme's `surface` fill, its corner radius, a
hairline edge in its `line` token, and — the part that saves the wrapper view — a
**default layout that stacks and pads**, so a Card's children are written as a plain
list and arrive inset from every edge.

```declare
Card [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 280,
    Text [ fontSize = 15, fontWeight = semibold, text = "Storage" ],
    Text [ width = 100%, wrap = true,
        text = "Cards size themselves to what they hold, padding included." ],
    Divider [ ],
    Button [ label = "Manage" ]
    ]
```

**Use `Text`, not `TextLabel`, as a direct child.** A `TextLabel` positions itself
vertically — that cap-centering `y` is the whole of what it adds — and the card's layout
places the children it holds, so the two would own the same slot. `TextLabel` belongs
inside a control or a row that leaves `y` alone; a card's own lines are `Text`.

**Give it a width, not a height.** A Card has no height of its own: it takes the one its
content needs, `padding` counted in — so the box grows and shrinks with what is inside it,
and nothing has to be kept in step by hand. Set `height` only to cap it (with `clip = true`
if the content may exceed the cap).

**The inset is the card's own `padding` = 16**, a `View` attribute: the card has an
*inside*, and every child — stacked by the default layout, placing itself at `x = center`,
or opted out with `ignoreLayout` — is positioned from it. The **default arrangement** is
`SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 8 ]`, which simply stacks inside that room, 8 apart.

**Replacing the layout does not replace the padding.** Naming your own `layout:` member —
a `WrappingLayout`, a row, a stack with different numbers — overrides the arrangement
only; the card is still inset, because the inset was never the arrangement's. Write
`padding = 0` to take it away, or `padding = 24` for more. `fill`, `cornerRadius` and
`stroke` are ordinary defaults a use site may write over the same way.

## fill
The theme's `surface` — the token that means "the plane content sits on", one step off
the window's own `bg` in every preset, light and dark. Write over it for a card that
should sink into the page rather than rise off it (`fill = { theme.bg }`), and pair that
with a kept `stroke` so the shape still reads.

## cornerRadius
The theme's `controlRadius`, so a card's corners match the buttons and fields inside it
— 7 in the house look, 4 in `Redmond` and `MountainView`. Set a number to break with the
preset; the box still clips nothing unless you also say `clip = true`.

## theme
The provided design-token record the card styles off. Reads `surface` for the fill, `line`
for the edge, and `controlRadius` for the corners — the tokens every preset carries, so a
card is the theme's surface in all four, light and dark. See `Control.theme`.

## stroke
A one-pixel edge in the theme's `line`. **`stroke = null` removes it** — the right move
for a card that already stands off the page by fill alone, or one nested inside another.
Per-side works here as anywhere: `stroke = [ null, null, stroke(1, #DBE1E9), null ]` turns
a card into a row with a rule under it.
*/

include [ "control.declare" ]

class Card extends View [
    // the design-token record, a provided value: the app provides it once and
    // this widget reads it. Default = the house look.

    theme: Theme = { provided("theme", SanFrancisco) },

    fill = { theme.surface },
    cornerRadius = { theme.controlRadius },
    stroke = { stroke(1, theme.line) },

    // The inset that saves the wrapper view: the card's own content box, so
    // every child's x/y is measured from inside it — the stacked ones, and a
    // child that places itself across the card alike. The inset is the view's,
    // not the arrangement's, which is what lets a child ignore the layout and
    // still sit inside the padding.

    padding = 16,

    // The default arrangement — a plain stack, inside the room above.

    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 8 ]
    ]
